    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.listening_items, $seed${"slug":"c1-hear-why-a-bus-shelter-pilot-was-paused","title":"Hear why a bus shelter pilot was paused","level":"C1","status":"published","sort_order":200,"metadata":{"format":"dialogue","script_type":"dialogue","speakers":["Host","Planner"]},"summary":"A planning interview separates a design decision from an incomplete evaluation.","transcript_text":"Host: The shelter pilot seems to have failed. Two stops have already gone back to their old layouts.\nPlanner: Those stops were chosen because they flood. Then a nearby road closure doubled the number of buses turning past them. Drivers had trouble seeing through the new side panel after dark.\nHost: Would a clear panel solve that?\nPlanner: It might. A clear panel produces glare under some streetlights. We have built a full-sized model so drivers and wheelchair users can try alternatives under controlled lighting. After that, we'll take the most promising version back to a stop. I also want to test it on a road that hasn't been affected by the closure.\nHost: So taking out the panels was one decision. The final design is still open.","category":"Community","accent":"general","speed_label":"natural","speed_wpm":145,"duration_seconds":51,"audio_url":null,"focus_skill":"inference","tags":["community","dialogue"],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What distinction does the planner make?","options":["An immediate removal differs from a final design decision","Only flooded sites should ever be tested","A mock-up can replace every field test","An interim solution always becomes permanent"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"The panels came out, but the planner is still investigating designs.","focus":"gist"},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"How did the road closure complicate interpretation?","options":["It disconnected the shelter lights","It made the drivers avoid both routes","It flooded the trial sites directly","It changed the traffic going past the shelters"],"answer_index":3,"explanation":"The closure increased turning traffic.","focus":"detail"},{"id":"q3","type":"mcq","prompt":"What risk comes with a transparent panel?","options":["Reduced visibility in all conditions","No driver can see through it at all","Light reflections in some conditions","It floods before the opaque version"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"The planner identifies glare as a conditional issue.","focus":"detail"},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why use the full-sized model?","options":["It makes field sites unnecessary","Users can compare options under controlled lighting","It removes the need to ask drivers","It guarantees flood resistance"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"The model lets users test alternatives under deliberately set conditions.","focus":"detail"},{"id":"q5","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why test beyond the affected road?","options":["To separate some traffic effects from layout effects","To avoid evaluating turning buses","To make the different panels impossible to compare","To prove changes always improve safety"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"A second site reduces the influence of the unusual diversion.","focus":"detail"}]}$seed$::jsonb) r
