    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.speaking_prompts, $seed${"slug":"b1-applaud-at-the-end-of-the-recital","title":"Tell About a Misread Concert Cue","level":"B1","status":"published","sort_order":375,"metadata":{"task_type":"story","cue_keywords":[["pause","stopped","hands stayed","went quiet"],["clap","still playing","started again","final section"],["now","look at the player","bow","wait"]],"model_answer":"I went to a student recital last spring. One player stopped after a loud part, and the room went quiet. I thought the tune was finished and began to clap. Then I saw that the player's hands were still in place. She looked at the music and started again. There was one short section left, and I felt a bit silly because I had stopped listening at the very moment it became quiet. Now, if I'm not sure a piece has ended, I wait and look at the player. A pause on its own doesn't tell me as much as what the player does next.","model_opening":"I went to a student recital last spring."},"prompt_text":"At a student recital, you thought a short pause meant the tune had finished. You began to clap, then realised the player still had a final section to play. Tell someone who was not there what led to the mistake, what you noticed next, and how you now recognise the end of a piece.","prompt_type":"storytelling","target_duration_seconds":67,"target_grammar":"Past simple and past continuous, then and when for sequence, and present simple for a habit.","target_vocabulary":["recital","pause","applaud","final section","bow"],"fluency_objective":"Make the pause a clear turning point. Add one direct observation before the reflection.","pronunciation_objective":"Slow down briefly around the pause to make the story clear. Avoid copying the sound of the performance.","cue_points":["Describe what made the pause look like the end.","Describe what happened after you clapped.","Say what you look for now."]}$seed$::jsonb) r
