    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.grammar_units, $seed${"slug":"a1-wh-questions-with-be-at-reception","title":"Who, what and where questions at sports center reception","level":"A1","status":"published","sort_order":370,"metadata":{},"focus_area":"wh-questions-with-be","explanation_text":"At a sports center, use who to ask about a person, what to ask about a thing or information, and where to ask for a location. When the question already names the person or thing you are asking about, put the question word first, then am, is or are, then the person or thing. The receptionist can ask “What is your name?” A visitor can ask “Where are the lockers?” or “Where am I on this map?”\n\nSometimes who is the subject. It replaces the person you want to know about. Keep the rest of the sentence in the same order. For example, “Mina is here for yoga” becomes “Who is here for yoga?” In “Who is your coach?”, the named subject is your coach, and the question asks who fills that role.","contrast_text":"“Who is ready for the swim test?” asks which person is ready. Who is the subject. In “Who is your swim teacher?”, your swim teacher is the subject. The question asks who fills the role.","common_error_pattern":"Putting am, is or are after the named subject in a direct question, using the wrong form of be, or adding he or she when who is already the subject.","examples_json":[{"bad":"Where the pool is?","good":"Where is the pool?"},{"bad":"What are your phone number?","good":"What is your phone number?"},{"bad":"Where is I in the line?","good":"Where am I in the line?"},{"bad":"Who is she ready for the tennis lesson?","good":"Who is ready for the tennis lesson?"}],"tags":["wh-questions","be","subject-questions","sports-center-reception"]}$seed$::jsonb) r
