    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.grammar_units, $seed${"slug":"a1-whose-and-possessive-pronouns","title":"Whose is this? Finding owners after class","level":"A1","status":"published","sort_order":380,"metadata":{},"focus_area":"whose-and-possessive-pronouns","explanation_text":"Class is over at the community center. There is a bag on the table. Ask “Whose bag is this?” to find the owner. Use whose before the name of the thing: whose bag, whose pen, whose book. If everyone can see the bag you mean, you can point to it and ask “Whose is this?”\n\nIf the bag belongs to you, you can say “It’s my bag” or “It’s mine.” Use my, your, her, our and their before a noun. Use mine, yours, hers, ours and theirs when the noun is already clear. For example, if Eva says “It’s my bag,” you can say “It’s hers.” If you speak directly to Eva, say “It’s yours.” If the bag belongs to you and your friend, say “It’s ours.” If it belongs to two other people, say “It’s theirs.”","contrast_text":"Whose notebook is this? → Whose is this? | It’s my notebook. → It’s mine. | It’s your notebook. → It’s yours. | It’s her notebook. → It’s hers. | It’s our notebook. → It’s ours. | It’s their notebook. → It’s theirs.","common_error_pattern":"Using a possessive pronoun before a noun, as in “mine bag,” or ending a sentence with a form that needs a noun, as in “It’s my.”","examples_json":[{"bad":"Whose is this pencil belongs to?","good":"Whose pencil is this?"},{"bad":"Whose this is?","good":"Whose is this?"},{"bad":"That’s mine coat.","good":"That’s my coat."},{"bad":"The lunch box is her.","good":"The lunch box is hers."}],"tags":["A1","whose","possessive-pronouns","ownership"]}$seed$::jsonb) r
