    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.grammar_units, $seed${"slug":"a1-would-like-at-a-food-counter","title":"Would like at a food counter","level":"A1","status":"published","sort_order":400,"metadata":{},"focus_area":"would-like-and-id-like","explanation_text":"At a food counter, start your request with “I'd like.” I'd like is the short form of I would like. Put the food or drink right after like. For example, say “I'd like a muffin, please” or “I would like some milk, please.” You can add please at the end of your request.\n\nIf you work at the counter, start an offer with “Would you like”. Put the food or drink after like and say, for example, “Would you like a cookie?” The word order changes because this is a question. Use to after like when the next word is an action, as in “I'd like to sit here.” With a food or drink, say “I'd like a salad.”\n\n“Would you like some tea?” offers tea now. “Do you like tea?” asks how someone feels about tea in general.","contrast_text":"“Would you like some tea?” means I am offering you tea now. “Do you like tea?” asks if you enjoy tea in general. Compare “I'd like a juice” for a drink and “I'd like to pay” for an action.","common_error_pattern":"Adding to before the food or drink, as in “I'd like to a sandwich.” Put the food or drink directly after like. Use to before an action verb, as in “I'd like to order.”","examples_json":[{"bad":"I'd like to a sandwich, please.","good":"I'd like a sandwich, please."},{"bad":"I would likes some milk, please.","good":"I would like some milk, please."},{"bad":"Would like you a cookie?","good":"Would you like a cookie?"},{"bad":"I'd like pay now.","good":"I'd like to pay now."}],"tags":["A1","would-like","polite-requests","offers","food-counter"]}$seed$::jsonb) r
