    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.learning_content_registry_items, $seed${"service_key":"grammar","content_type":"grammar.unit","source_table":"grammar_units","source_ref":"a2-before-after-and-when-in-instructions","title":"Before, after and when in practice instructions","skill_key":"grammar","cefr_level":"A2","difficulty":0.3,"status":"published","description":"Use before, after or when to join two parts of an instruction. In this pattern, put a subject and a verb after the time word. In “before you practise”, you is the subject and practise is the verb. Before tells you that the main action comes first. After tells you that the action in the time clause comes first. When tells you at what point to do the main action. Use the present simple in the time clause.\n\nStart with these two steps: “Write a title. Read the questions.” You can join them as “Write a title before you read the questions” or “After you write a title, read the questions.” The order of the actions stays the same in both sentences.\n\nA time clause can go at the beginning or the end. “When you reach the next page, stop” gives the same instruction as “Stop when you reach the next page.” Put a comma after a time clause at the beginning.","subskill_keys":["grammar.accuracy","grammar.before-after-when-clauses","grammar.transfer"],"estimated_minutes":8,"learning_objective":"Two short instructions can show the steps in the order you do them. When you join the steps, choose the time word that keeps that order. For the steps “Number the lines. Read the text,” you can say “Number the lines before you read the text” or “After you number the lines, read the text.” You can also move the same time clause without changing the order of the actions: “Before you read the text, number the lines.”","tags":["time-clauses","before-after-when","sequencing-instructions"],"payload_json":{"focus_area":"before-after-when-clauses","common_error_pattern":"Learners may leave out the subject or verb after before, after or when, or use will in the time clause. They may also switch before and after without checking which action comes first.","examples":[{"bad":"Set out the cards before you the game.","good":"Set out the cards before you start the game."},{"bad":"After finish the puzzle, count your points.","good":"After you finish the puzzle, count your points."},{"bad":"When the music will stop, turn over your paper.","good":"When the music stops, turn over your paper."},{"bad":"Required order: choose a partner first, then move the chairs. “Move the chairs before you choose a partner” gives the opposite order.","good":"Move the chairs after you choose a partner."}]}}$seed$::jsonb) r
