    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.learning_content_registry_items, $seed${"service_key":"grammar","content_type":"grammar.unit","source_table":"grammar_units","source_ref":"a2-too-also-and-negative-either","title":"Too, also and negative either in shared lunch plans","skill_key":"grammar","cefr_level":"A2","difficulty":0.3,"status":"published","description":"When friends plan a lunch together, they often have the same idea or add another job to the list. Use too at the end of a positive sentence to show that something is true for another person or that someone plans to do one more thing. For example, “I can bring bread.” “I can bring bread too.” Here, too means “as well.” In “The soup is too hot,” too has a different meaning. This lesson is about adding information.\n\nIn a simple affirmative sentence with an ordinary main verb, put also before that verb: “We also need cups.” With can, put also after can: “We can also bring cups.” With am, is or are as the main verb, put also after it, as in “The small table is also free.” To agree with a negative statement or add another negative point, use either at the end. For example, “I don’t need a fork.” “I don’t need one either.” The second sentence still needs a negative word such as don’t.","subskill_keys":["grammar.accuracy","grammar.addition-too-also-either","grammar.transfer"],"estimated_minutes":8,"learning_objective":"“I want soup too” and “I also want soup” add a positive point. “I don’t want soup either” adds a negative point. “The soup is too salty” means it has more salt than you want.","tags":["A2","addition","adverb-position","negative-agreement","shared-lunch"],"payload_json":{"focus_area":"addition-too-also-either","common_error_pattern":"In these basic patterns, learners may put also before am, is or are, use too to agree with a negative statement, or use either without keeping the second statement negative.","examples":[{"bad":"I want a slice of too bread.","good":"I want a slice of bread too."},{"bad":"We need to bring a serving also spoon.","good":"We also need to bring a serving spoon."},{"bad":"The back room is free. The front also room is free.","good":"The back room is free. The front room is also free."},{"bad":"Context: Both people want to skip dessert. “I don’t want dessert.” “I want dessert too.”","good":"“I don’t want dessert.” “I don’t want dessert either.”"}]}}$seed$::jsonb) r
