    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.learning_content_registry_items, $seed${"service_key":"grammar","content_type":"grammar.unit","source_table":"grammar_units","source_ref":"b2-emphatic-do-to-correct-a-record","title":"Emphatic do to correct a record","skill_key":"grammar","cefr_level":"B2","difficulty":0.6,"status":"published","description":"Use do, does or did in an affirmative statement when you want to insist that a claim is true or correct someone’s assumption. If a teammate thinks you skipped the opening scene, you can say, “We did rehearse the opening scene.” In speech, stress did. The stress lets the listener hear that you are pushing back against the idea that the rehearsal did not happen.\n\nKeep the original subject and tense. In the present simple, use does with he, she, it or a singular name, and do with I, you, we or they. In the past simple, use did with any subject. Put the base form of the main verb after the auxiliary: “Mina does check” and “Mina did check.” In a yes/no question, the auxiliary comes before the subject: “Did Mina check the list?” An emphatic statement keeps the subject first: “Mina did check the list.”","subskill_keys":["grammar.accuracy","grammar.emphatic-do","grammar.transfer"],"estimated_minutes":8,"learning_objective":"“They checked the props” states what happened. “They did check the props” stresses that it happened, for example when the activity log says the check was skipped. “Did they check the props?” asks whether it happened.","tags":["emphatic-do","affirmative-statements","correcting-assumptions","sentence-stress"],"payload_json":{"focus_area":"emphatic-do","common_error_pattern":"Putting a past ending or third-person -s on the main verb after did or does. The auxiliary carries the tense and any subject agreement, so the main verb stays in its base form.","examples":[{"bad":"We did rehearsed the final scene.","good":"We did rehearse the final scene."},{"bad":"Hana does checks the prop list before each rehearsal.","good":"Hana does check the prop list before each rehearsal."},{"bad":"The dancers does warm up before practice.","good":"The dancers do warm up before practice."},{"bad":"Did the crew test the lights? (A question when you mean to correct the claim that they skipped the test.)","good":"The crew did test the lights."}]}}$seed$::jsonb) r
