    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.learning_content_registry_items, $seed${"service_key":"grammar","content_type":"grammar.unit","source_table":"grammar_units","source_ref":"c1-topic-fronting-without-inversion","title":"Topic fronting without inversion","skill_key":"grammar","cefr_level":"C1","difficulty":0.75,"status":"published","description":"Put an object or complement first when you want to make it the topic of a contrast. Move the whole phrase, including any words that identify it, and leave its usual position empty. Keep the subject before the verb or auxiliary: ‘The request for a second microphone we can accommodate.’ Here, ‘we’ still identifies who can act. You can also front a complement, as in ‘“Final” I would not call this draft.’ The negative stays with the verb, and ‘this draft’ stays in its usual position.\n\nTopic fronting often sounds emphatic in speech and is marked in formal prose. In short minutes, use it sparingly when the contrast matters: ‘The request for step-free access the board approved. The request for a later start the treasurer contested.’ A fronted negative adverbial follows a different pattern: ‘At no point did the treasurer withdraw her objection.’ In that sentence, the auxiliary ‘did’ comes before","subskill_keys":["grammar.accuracy","grammar.topic-fronting","grammar.transfer"],"estimated_minutes":8,"learning_objective":"In ‘The revised estimate I cannot accept’, the fronted object names what is under discussion and ‘I’ precedes ‘cannot’. In ‘Under no circumstances can I accept the revised estimate’, the fronted negative adverbial triggers inversion, so ‘can’ precedes ‘I’.","tags":["C1","topic-fronting","contrastive-topic","word-order","register"],"payload_json":{"focus_area":"topic-fronting","common_error_pattern":"Learners sometimes add question-style inversion after a fronted object, repeat the object with ‘it’, or leave behind words that identify the intended topic. Move the complete object or complement and keep the original subject in front of its verb or auxiliary.","examples":[{"bad":"The request for a larger room did the board approve, but the request for extra staffing the treasurer contested.","good":"The request for a larger room the board approved, but the request for extra staffing the treasurer contested."},{"bad":"The revised caption I can publish it today, but the original needs a fact-check.","good":"The revised caption I can publish today, but the original needs a fact-check."},{"bad":"‘Ready for release’ would I not call this version, but it is ready for internal review.","good":"‘Ready for release’ I would not call this version, but it is ready for internal review."},{"bad":"At no point the surveyor did endorse the estimate.","good":"At no point did the surveyor endorse the estimate."}]}}$seed$::jsonb) r
