      jsonb_build_object('seeded_by', v_seed)
    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.learning_content_registry_items, $seed${"service_key":"grammar","content_type":"grammar.unit","source_table":"grammar_units","source_ref":"c2-reflexive-arguments-and-emphasis","title":"Reflexive arguments and emphasis","skill_key":"grammar","cefr_level":"C2","difficulty":0.9,"status":"published","description":"A reflexive can occupy an object slot and refer back to the subject of its own clause. In “Nora blamed herself,” herself is the direct object and identifies the person Nora blamed. In “The trustees granted themselves a waiver,” themselves is the indirect object and identifies the recipients. If Nora says that the trustees blamed themselves, the reflexive refers to the trustees, the subject of the embedded clause.\n\nAn intensifying reflexive adds emphasis and can be removed without leaving an object slot empty. “Rina herself signed” singles out Rina when a deputy was expected to sign. “Rina signed it herself” stresses that she performed the act personally, as when someone asks whether she delegated the signing. These two readings can overlap.\n\n“Rina signed by herself” can mean that she signed while alone or that she signed without assistance. If everyone had left the room, the context supp","subskill_keys":["grammar.accuracy","grammar.reflexive-arguments-and-emphasis","grammar.transfer"],"estimated_minutes":8,"learning_objective":"In “Leila assigned herself a locker,” herself is the recipient. In “Leila herself assigned the lockers,” herself singles out Leila as the person who assigned them. In “Leila assigned the lockers herself,” herself emphasizes that Leila did the assigning personally. In “Leila assigned the lockers by herself,” by herself adds that she was alone or worked without help, depending on the context.","tags":["reflexive-arguments","direct-and-indirect-objects","intensifying-reflexives","by-oneself"],"payload_json":{"focus_area":"reflexive-arguments-and-emphasis","common_error_pattern":"Using an ordinary object pronoun for a direct or indirect object that refers back to the subject of the same clause, or choosing the reflexive to match the subject of a higher clause. A separate error is assuming that a bare intensifier guarantees solitude or lack of assistance.","examples":[{"bad":"Mina blamed her for the mix-up. (Intended meaning: Mina blamed Mina.)","good":"Mina blamed herself for the mix-up."},{"bad":"The panelists allotted them ten extra minutes. (Intended meaning: The panelists allotted the time to the panelists.)","good":"The panelists allotted themselves ten extra minutes."},{"bad":"The director reported that the conservators had photographed herself for the staff archive.","good":"The director reported that the conservators had photographed themselves for the staff archive."},{"bad":"Rina signed it herself. (Contextual mismatch: The audit field asks whether anyone else was in the room.)","good":"Rina signed by herself in the empty room."}]}}$seed$::jsonb) r
