    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.learning_content_registry_items, $seed${"service_key":"grammar","content_type":"grammar.unit","source_table":"grammar_units","source_ref":"c2-the-more-the-less-and-linked-change","title":"Comparative correlatives and linked change","skill_key":"grammar","cefr_level":"C2","difficulty":0.9,"status":"published","description":"Use a comparative correlative to show how one variable changes in relation to another. In “The fewer steps the process has, the less time it takes,” each clause begins with the followed by a comparative phrase. The rest of each clause usually follows ordinary subject–verb order in neutral prose. Put the variable you are using as a reference point in the first clause and the corresponding change in the second.\n\nThe directions can differ: a shorter transfer window may go with more missed connections. Use fewer with plural count nouns such as steps and less with noncount nouns such as time. You can also link one change to two outcomes, as in “The clearer the catalogue entries are, the fewer requests staff have to clarify and the less time they spend checking references.”\n\nIn a report, use “In the records reviewed” or a similar phrase to limit the claim to the evidence. The construction expr","subskill_keys":["grammar.accuracy","grammar.comparative-correlatives","grammar.transfer"],"estimated_minutes":8,"learning_objective":"“In the dispatch logs, the later the feeder bus arrived, the more passengers missed the last train” reports a pattern. “The feeder bus’s late arrival caused passengers to miss the last train” attributes the missed connections to that delay. Choose fewer missed connections when counting events and less waiting time when measuring duration.","tags":["c2-the-more-the-less-and-linked-change","comparative-correlatives","linked-change","count-and-noncount","correlation-and-causation"],"payload_json":{"focus_area":"comparative-correlatives","common_error_pattern":"Omitting the before the second comparative, using less with a plural count noun such as files, or importing question word order as in “the longer did the layover last”.","examples":[{"bad":"The more precise the shelf marks are, less time readers spend locating the folders.","good":"The more precise the shelf marks are, the less time readers spend locating the folders."},{"bad":"The longer the gap between trains is, the less departures the schedule can accommodate.","good":"The longer the gap between trains is, the fewer departures the schedule can accommodate."},{"bad":"The longer did the layover last, the more often dispatchers reassigned bays.","good":"The longer the layover lasted, the more often dispatchers reassigned bays."},{"bad":"Evidence mismatch (the survey recorded co-variation only): Increasing the interval between trams caused platform queues to grow.","good":"In the survey, the longer the interval between trams was, the longer the platform queues were."}]}}$seed$::jsonb) r
